  </read_first>
  <action>
    Confirmed live on this machine (2026-09-10): `uv run pytest
    tests/test_version_status.py -k test_fresh_manager_snapshot_issues_zero_queries -q` fails
    with `assert 'unknown' == 'pnpm'` on `statuses["mmdc"]`, and D-03 (12.1-CONTEXT.md) records
    the same failure reproducing on an unmodified origin `b9a8876` checkout — pre-existing, not
    introduced by the reconciliation. Root cause: this machine's real `PNPM_HOME`
    (`/home/bazzite/.local/share/pnpm`) leaks into `installer/ownership.py::owner_dirs`, which
    reads `os.environ.get("PNPM_HOME")` unconditionally, while the test's `_which_for_fixture`
    hardcodes a macOS-shaped `~/Library/pnpm/mmdc` active path — a path that only matches
    `owner_dirs`'s OWN platform-derived default (`_PNPM_DEFAULTS["macos"]`) when the real env
    var happens to be unset. `tests/test_ownership.py` already isolates this exact env var via
    `monkeypatch`; `test_version_status.py` never adopted that pattern for its
    `_node_tool()`/`_which_for_fixture` fixtures.

    Add `import pytest` to `tests/test_version_status.py`'s import block (it currently imports
    no pytest symbol at all). Add a `monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch` parameter to
    `test_fresh_manager_snapshot_issues_zero_queries`, and as its first two statements call
    `monkeypatch.delenv("PNPM_HOME", raising=False)` and `monkeypatch.delenv("UV_TOOL_BIN_DIR",
